  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1137 PM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nPrecip chances... High pressure under building ridge will keep \npersistent dry conditions in the forecast through mid-week. Our \nnext chances for light rain then arrive late Wednesday into \nThursday as upper trough residing over the western CONUS ejects \ninto the upper Midwest, dragging with it a surface low aimed for \nthe James Bay. Forcing along the associated cold front looks \nrelatively weak as better moisture and dynamics remain holed up \nnear the surface low, so probabilities for receiving 0.1\" of QPF \ncurrently only stand at around 20 to 40% across the forecast area.\n\nNext chances for more widespread precip arrive Friday into\nSaturday as a more robust trough tilts up into the Midwest,\npotentially becoming closed off as the attendant surface low \noccludes over Canada. This system looks to have a bit more\nmoisture with it as southerly winds tap into Gulf airmass, with\nPWATs nearing around 1\" Saturday afternoon as a result. However,\nthere is still lots of uncertainty regarding total QPF, as most \nrecent suite of ensembles have trended the system further north.\nIf the northerly solution were to pan out, this would\nsignificantly cut into rainfall amounts across northeast \nWisconsin. On the other end, more southerly solution would \nintroduce potential for higher-end QPF amounts and a rain/snow mix\nas cooler air wraps in around the back of the departing low. As it\nstands now, dominant ptype would be rain, with probabilistic\nguidance showing a 30 to 60% signal for 0.5\" of total rainfall.\nWill continue to monitor trends over the course of the week.\n\nTemperatures... Following a brief period of near average\ntemperatures, gradual warming trend sets in to start the work \nweek as southerly/return flow takes hold. When all is said and \ndone, highs will once again be flirting with 60 degrees at some\nlocations toward the end of the week, warmest on Thursday and\nSaturday. Record highs look safe, though some record high mins may\nbe in jeopardy.",
